speechtext:
From Truro. He has been in the service thirty or thirty-five years. I think there is no man in the Customs Department better qualified or more able to handle a position of that kind than Mr. Blair. Mr. Blair himself in August, 1914, at the time of tlie outbreak of the war, was very anxious to go to the front; in fact he asked me and urged me to allow him to go, and also applied to General Hughes. He was not allowed to go because he was over age and because we could not spare him at that time. We had educated him as to the needs of the department; he had been given a\l necessary instructions as to how to handle the Customs business in case war was declared. He was the man who went from Sydney to Vancouver months before the outbreak of war instructing the collectors so that they would know how to act if war was declared. Mr. Blair sent his own son to the front, and to-day he lies buried in France, a wife and small child surviving him. I sent Mr. Blair to Toronto because he was the man who was qualified to go there. He was feeling badly over the loss of his son. I sent Mr. Bertram from Toronto to Montreal; he is one of the best officials in Canada. It required a man like Mr. Bertram or Mr. Blair to take charge of that office at that particular time. Any one who knows Mr. Bertram will say that no other man in Canada is better qualified than he is. My understanding of the Order in Council is that if a returned soldier is entitled to a position as_ an officer of Customs at a salary of $200, he is entitled to a position in the city of Montreal at $4,500. I have, therefore, taken the same position in respect to this office as I have in respect'to the other offices in Canada now vacant and which I am holding. I have sent men from other offices to fill positions temporarily until I can get men who have served at the front and who I believe could fill the positions. I have sent Mr. Bertram to Montreal as collector, and he is filling the position well. In these war times it requires a man of his experience or that of Air. Blair's to fill that position temporarily,
. ;

until ail appointment is finally made. If I were to appoint a man other than one who has had experience, it would be necessary to have a man like Mr. Blair or Mr. Bertram work with him for a few months in order to educate him so that he might discharge his duties efficiently. In Montreal we collect $35,000,000 per annum-I think that was the amount collected last year. Most of the shipments from Canada pass through that port. I have stated the position that I took when vacancies occurred, and that is tlbe position I am going to take until I can get men from the front. I know most of the men in Montreal who have gone to the front, and I believe that any man who goes1 to the front and does ihis duty for his country should get that position as weill as any other.
